abstract: >-
  A controlled comparison of how well LLM judges detect faults in tool-using
  agent trajectories. A synthetic support-desk environment with a scripted
  oracle policy provides known-correct behaviour; a six-type fault injector
  then breaks exactly one step at a known index and records whether the
  customer-visible outcome survived, which stratifies failures into silent
  and loud. Five judges — a programmatic rule checker, an outcome-only judge,
  a step-rubric judge at two model sizes, and a self-consistency ensemble —
  are scored on detection, step localisation, failure-type classification,
  calibration and cost. The outcome-only judge, which is the production
  default, recovers 0.84 of faults that broke the answer but only 0.45 of
  those that did not, while raising false alarms on a third of correct
  trajectories. Every raw verdict is committed so the tables rebuild offline.
